The issue

You mentioned:

When I input a wrong username or password, it failed to get the /auth/failureLogin page.

This usually points to one of a few things.

1. Check your login controller first

I'd like to see the code that handles login. It should have logic similar to:

const user = await User.findOne({ username });

if (!user) {
    return res.redirect("/auth/failureLogin");
}

const isMatch = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password);

if (!isMatch) {
    return res.redirect("/auth/failureLogin");
}

If either of those return statements is missing, Express will continue executing the rest of the function.

2. Verify the route exists

In your auth routes, ensure you have something like:

router.get("/failureLogin", (req, res) => {
    res.render("auth/failureLogin");
});

If this route doesn't exist, Express will return a 404.

3. Check the filename

Verify the file is actually named exactly:

views/

views/
    auth/
        failureLogin.ejs

Remember that Linux servers (like Render) are case-sensitive.

These are different:

failureLogin.ejs ✅
FailureLogin.ejs ❌
failurelogin.ejs ❌

4. Check the redirect path

If your route is

router.get("/failureLogin", ...)

and the router is mounted as

app.use("/auth", authRoutes);

then the redirect must be

res.redirect("/auth/failureLogin");

not

res.redirect("failureLogin");

or

res.redirect("/failureLogin");

5. Is Passport involved?

If you're using Passport Local Strategy, the login route usually looks like:

router.post(
    "/login",
    passport.authenticate("local", {
        successRedirect: "/",
        failureRedirect: "/auth/failureLogin"
    })
);

In that case, the problem could be inside the Passport strategy instead of your controller.

