That observation is actually a very useful clue. It suggests your routing is behaving exactly as Express is configured to behave.

Here's what's likely happening.

If in your app.js you have something like:

app.use("/auth", authRoutes);

then every route inside authRoutes.js automatically gets /auth prefixed to it.

So if authRoutes.js contains:

router.get("/premium", ...);

the actual URL becomes:

/auth/premium

—not—

/premium

The same applies to:

router.get("/failureLogin", ...);

which becomes:

/auth/failureLogin
Why /premium didn't work

If you tried visiting:

/premium

Express looked for a route defined directly in app.js or another router mounted at /, didn't find one, and returned a 404 (or your invalid page).

When you switched to:

/auth/premium

it matched the mounted router, so it worked.

This also ties back to your login issue

If your login controller redirects like this:

res.redirect("/failureLogin");

it will fail because that route doesn't exist.

It should be:

res.redirect("/auth/failureLogin");

assuming failureLogin lives inside authRoutes.js.

There are two valid ways to organize this
Option 1 (Recommended)

Keep authentication-related pages together:

/auth/login
/auth/register
/auth/logout
/auth/premium
/auth/failureLogin

This is clean and RESTful. Many production apps are organized this way.

Option 2

If you want Premium to be a site-wide page instead of an auth page, move it to another router.

Example:

// app.js
app.use("/", indexRoutes);
app.use("/auth", authRoutes);

Then in indexRoutes.js:

router.get("/premium", ...);

Now the URL becomes:

/premium

which is also perfectly valid.

My recommendation for Prof Predict

Based on what we've reviewed over the past few sessions, I'd organize it like this:

/
├── /
├── /about
├── /contact
├── /predictions
├── /premium      ← public page describing premium features
│
└── /auth
    ├── /login
    ├── /register
    ├── /logout
    ├── /failureLogin
    └── /dashboard (if needed)

That way, Premium is part of the main application, while authentication routes stay grouped under /auth.

So far, your observations don't point to a bug in Express—they point to route organization. Once I see your authRoutes.js and app.js, we can confirm whether the login redirect issue is simply the same routing pattern or something different in the login controller.

