Step 4 — Lock Down Prediction Routes

These routes should be protected:

POST /predictions/create
GET /predictions/edit/:id
POST /predictions/edit/:id
POST /predictions/delete/:id

All of them should use:

requireAdmin

Regular users should only be able to view predictions.

Step 5 — Professional Error Pages

Create:
views/
    403.ejs
    404.ejs
    500.ejs

Instead of showing stack traces, users will see friendly error pages.

Examples:

403 → "You don't have permission to access this page."
404 → "The page you're looking for doesn't exist."
500 → "Something went wrong. Please try again later."
Step 6 — Better Session Checks

After login, save only the information you need in the session:

req.session.user = {
    id: user._id,
    username: user.username,
    role: user.role
};

Avoid storing the entire user document, especially the hashed password or other unnecessary fields.

Step 7 — Input Validation

Never trust incoming data.

For example:

if (!country || !league || !homeTeam || !awayTeam) {
    return res.status(400).render("prediction/new", {
        error: "All fields are required."
    });
}

This complements your Mongoose validation by catching issues before attempting to save to the database.

Step 8 — Production Security

Before deployment, add:

helmet for secure HTTP headers.
express-rate-limit to reduce brute-force attacks.
csurf (or another CSRF protection approach compatible with your setup) for form protection.
Secure session cookie settings (httpOnly, sameSite, secure in production).
Environment-based configuration for secrets and cookie settings.

These are standard practices for Express applications exposed to the public internet.

